I'll work through this problem using the theory of Lucas sequences, computing the rank of apparition for each prime below 50.

## Solution

The sequence $a_n$ is the Lucas sequence $U_n(P,Q)$ with $P=2014, Q=1$, satisfying $a_0=0, a_1=1, a_{n+2}=Pa_{n+1}-Qa_n$. The discriminant is $D = P^2-4Q = 2014^2-4 = 4056192 = 2^4\cdot 3^2\cdot 7\cdot 503$.

**Key fact:** For a prime $p$, let $\rho(p)$ denote the *rank of apparition* — the smallest $m\geq 1$ with $p\mid a_m$. Then $p\mid a_n \iff \rho(p)\mid n$. (For $p\nmid 2QD$ this is standard Lucas sequence theory; for $p\mid D$ with $p$ odd, $U_n\equiv n\cdot r^{n-1}\pmod p$ where $r=P/2$ is the double root, giving $\rho(p)=p$ when $\gcd(r,p)=1$; for $p=2$ we verify directly.)

**Computing $\rho(p)$ for each prime $p<50$** (using $a_{n+2}\equiv Pa_{n+1}-a_n\pmod p$):

| $p$ | $P\bmod p$ | $\rho(p)$ | | $p$ | $P\bmod p$ | $\rho(p)$ |
|-----|-----------|-----------|---|-----|-----------|-----------|
| 2 | 0 | 2 | | 29 | 13 | 14 |
| 3 | 1 | 3 | | 31 | $-1$ | 3 |
| 5 | $-1$ | 3 | | 37 | 16 | 18 |
| 7 | 5 | 7 | | 41 | 5 | 20 |
| 11 | 1 | 3 | | 43 | $-7$ | 11 |
| 13 | $-1$ | 3 | | 47 | $-7$ | 4 |
| 17 | 8 | 8 | | | | |
| 19 | 0 | 2 | | | | |
| 23 | 13 | 11 | | | | |

Each rank was verified by iterating the recurrence mod $p$ until reaching $a_{\rho}\equiv 0$ with $a_1\equiv 1$ at the preceding step (confirming a full period).

**Distilling the condition.** The set of distinct ranks is $\{2,3,4,7,8,11,14,18,20\}$. Since $4,8,14,18,20$ are all even, divisibility by any of them is subsumed by divisibility by $2$. Thus:

$$a_n \text{ is divisible by some prime}<50 \iff n\text{ is divisible by }2,\,3,\,7,\text{ or }11.$$

Equivalently, $a_n$ has **no** prime factor $<50$ iff $\gcd(n,\,2\cdot3\cdot7\cdot11)=\gcd(n,462)=1$.

**Finding the smallest $n\geq 2$:** Check $n=2,3,4,5$:
- $n=2$: $\gcd(2,462)=2\neq1$. Indeed $a_2=2014=2\times1007$. ✗
- $n=3$: $\gcd(3,462)=3\neq1$. Indeed $a_3=4056195=3\times1352065$. ✗
- $n=4$: $\gcd(4,462)=2\neq1$. Indeed $a_4$ is even. ✗
- $n=5$: $\gcd(5,462)=1$. ✓

**Direct verification for $n=5$.** Using $a_3=4056195$, $a_4=2014\cdot 4056194$, and $a_5=2014^2\cdot 4056194-4056195=(4056195^2-1)-4056195=4056195^2-4056195-1$. Setting $x=4056195$, we have $a_5=x^2-x-1$. Checking $a_5\bmod p$ for every prime $p<50$:

| $p$ | $x\bmod p$ | $a_5\bmod p$ | | $p$ | $x\bmod p$ | $a_5\bmod p$ |
|-----|-----------|-------------|---|-----|-----------|-------------|
| 2 | 1 | 1 | | 29 | 23 | 12 |
| 3 | 0 | 2 | | 31 | 0 | 30 |
| 5 | 0 | 4 | | 37 | 33 | 19 |
| 7 | 3 | 5 | | 41 | 24 | 18 |
| 11 | 0 | 10 | | 43 | 5 | 19 |
| 13 | 0 | 12 | | 47 | 1 | 46 |
| 17 | 12 | 12 | | | | |
| 19 | 18 | 1 | | | | |
| 23 | 7 | 18 | | | | |

Every residue is nonzero, confirming $a_5$ is not divisible by any prime less than 50.

## Answer

The smallest value of $n\geq 2$ for which $a_n$ is not divisible by any prime less than $50$ is $\boxed{5}$.

